Greetings,

Please bear with me as this is my first post to this newsgroup, and only my 
second post ever.  

Since this article is not strictly in reply to the ongoing posts about wands of
wishing and wishes in general, I am not including any previous articles.  (In
fact, the sole reason I am replying rather than posting an article a priori is
that I am unclear on how to post a priori).

It seems to me that there are many possible problems with implementing the 
game's primary wishing device as a wand.  Namely:

	1)    This wand may be created, albeit rarely, by polymorphing other 
	   wands.  This is, it seems to me, unbalancing, and is one of the
	   main reasons I am beginning to swing towards the anti-polymorph-
	   pile camp (getting named weapons, and item-producing items (non-
	   empty bags and chests) being the others).
	2)    The wand has a highly variable number of charges (I have found a
	   wand of wishing with just 1 charge (which is annoying) and I
	   gather that some exist with > 3 charges (or am I mistaken)) and
	   may be recharged (though I have found that attempting to recharge
	   the wand twice (ie. using all wishes, charging it, using up all 
	   those wishes, and then charging it again) causes it to explode.
	3)    It doesn't seem, to me, to fit the genre.  Why a *wand* of 
	   wishing?  Rings of wishing, magic lamps, genies freed from bottles,
	   demons from fountains (to a lesser degree) make sense in fantasy.
	   It seems to me that the sole reason it is a *wand* of wishing is 
	   that, probably, the original implementors wanted a charged item
	   which granted wishes, and wands were the only charged items (it's
	   how I, being a lazy CS student, would have done it :-)).
Sorry about all that rambling, but I wanted to explain what I feel to be the
main problems with wishing in Nethack 3.0.  I think that *every* player who 
manages to get through the castle deserves several wishes, but that the wishes
should be somewhat regulated and that a wand is a strange (subjectively 
speaking) device to grant wishes.
I purpose:

	a blessed _Alladin's Lamp_ (7)

The _Lamp_ would always be blessed and have 7 charges, but these charges would
NOT refer to the number of wishes, just the number of rooms the lamp can light.
Rather, the _Lamp_ would always contain a wish granting Djinn who would say 
something like:

	"Thank thee, O mortal, for granting my release.  I grant thee for thy
	 service three wishes!!!" (more)
	"What be thy first wish O' Master?" (more)
	"You may wish for an object."

The second (and third) time it is rubbed, the djinn will respond:

	"What be thy second (third) wish O' Master?" (more)...

After three wishes, the lamp remains an _Alladin's Lamp_, but the Djinn speaks
a final time and vanishes:

	"At last, I am free from the clutches of the Accursed Wizard!!!" (more)
	The Djinn vanishes in a puff of logic. 

Wishing for a _Lamp_ would, much like wishing for the amulet, gain one nothing
but a real-looking false _Alladin's Lamp_ which would just belch smoke when
rubbed.

	It seems to me that such a lamp might solve the biggest problems with
wishing, while maintaining the mystery (and portability) of the wand of 
wishing.

	Also, why not allow (certain) intrinsic abilities to be granted as a
wish?  Would this unfairly unbalance the game, if wishes could be restricted?
Note that such intrinsic abilities could, unlike those gained from items, be
stolen by, well, those ability-stealing monsters...  I welcome any input?

